  "strategy": "In this implementation, we pursue an approach centered around multi-resolved optimization and symmetric sliding window analysis. Our goal is to minimize the evaluation function by leveraging uniform initializations, geometric progressions, autocorrelation properties, and strategic length variances. The sheer diversity of our candidate sequences\u2014generated via different structural approaches\u2014enhances exploration while maintaining strict alignment with the non-negative constraint. \n\nHere's the complete plan:\n\n1. **Initialization**\n   - Load the initial sequence from `height_sequence_1` if available and valid\n   - If no initial sequence is available, start with a uniform sequence of length 256 (not 512) normalized by sum\n   - This uniform sequence aspect prevents us from prematurely focusing on any one strategy and potentially getting stuck.\n\n2. **Geometric Sequence Generation**\n   - Explicitly consider increasing sequence lengths beyond 512\n   - For each new distinct length, generate a candidate sequence via \n     linear interpolation (conservative scaling) or exponential scans (radical scaling)\n   - The sequences must be non-negative and normalized by sum\n\n3. **Autocorrelation-Optimized Checkpoints**\n   - Create checkpoints at lengths that are multiples of 512's prime factors (this ensures mathematical precision without overcomplication)\n   - For each checkpoint, optimize the sequence using a Radial Basis Function (RBF) approach to distribute energy and minimize peaks\n\n4. **Uniform Clipping Enforcement**\n   - Maintain all sequences within [0,1000] bounds (absolute enforcement from NumPy)\n   - Additionally enforce summation >= 0.01 to ensure valid evaluations\n\n5. **Extended Search Phases**\n   - Break the optimization into phases, each with a focused metric:\n     - Phase 1: Initial evaluation\n     - Phase 2: Additive spikes to test engagement (AFP)\n     - Phase 3: Bell-shaped distributions to test broad engagements (BSDE)\n     - Phase 4: Multi-modal tests with varying correlations (MMET)\n   - Each phase expands our knowledge base incrementally\n\n6. **Termination Safeguards**\n   - Pre-store the best candidate from each phase (evaluate via given function) and keep a record of minimal J values\n   - If time runs out before finding an optimal sequence, return the best available candidate\n\n7. **Complexity Analysis**\n   - Ensure each phase uses FFT for convolutions (time complexity ~ O(n log n))\n   - Intelligently reuse computations between phases (no redundant calculations)\n   - Manage RAM by processing one sequence at a time\n\n8. **Search Prototype Implementation**\n   - Import necessary libraries (numpy, scipy, scipy.special, etc.)\n   - Create an array-memory layout:\n     - Maintain a 3-dimensional storage for all candidate sequences (by normalized length)\n     - Process each normalized segment independently\n\nWith these steps, we ensure that our approach is methodical, respects the non-negative constraint strictly, and leverages mathematical organizational techniques to explore the solution space efficiently. It also avoids any direct negative consequences from advanced methodological incompleteness, primarily through strict adherence and careful incremental testing phases.",
